+note xcount: the x86-64 loop of cnt_word, after vs_chk. RSI = A + off (LEA of a global or text, else the
+note array's slot; ADD), RCX = n, AL = v (zero-extended uchar). XMM0 = v in all 16 byte lanes (MOVD,
+note PUNPCKLBW, PSHUFLW 0, PSHUFD 0), XMM3 = 0, EDX = 0 (the count). While RCX >= 16 (CMP; JB): a block of
+note R8 = min(RCX >> 4, 255) chunks (MOV, SHR, CMP, JBE, MOV R8D 255; PXOR XMM1), each chunk MOVDQU XMM2,
+note [RSI]; PCMPEQB XMM2, XMM0 (0xff where equal); PSUBB XMM1, XMM2 (+1 per equal lane; 255 chunks keep
+note each lane <= 255); ADD RSI, 16; SUB RCX, 16; DEC R8; JNE; then PSADBW XMM1, XMM3 (each 8-lane half
+note summed into its quadword), MOVQ R9 / ADD RDX twice around PSRLDQ XMM1, 8, JMP back. The last RCX < 16
+note bytes one at a time: TEST; JE; CMP [RSI], AL; JNE; INC RDX; INC RSI; DEC RCX; JNE. SSE2 only (every
+note x86-64 CPU), unaligned loads, never a byte outside the checked slice. The 128 bytes after the four
+note setup moves are fixed (Intel SDM vol. 2 encodings, checked byte for byte against llvm-mc 18; the short
+note jumps' distances are constant); RDX holds the result for cnt_word. RAX, RCX, RDX, RSI, R8, R9 and
+note XMM0-XMM3 are emitter scratch here: language values live in RBP slots.
